  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 219 AM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThe mid level troughing over the Southeast will gradually evolve \ninto a closed low on Monday night and it will be parked over the \nTennessee River Valley through Tuesday before gradually opening \nback up into a trough and getting pulled north towards the middle \nof the week. To the east, mid level ridging will remain centered \nover the western Atlantic. The southwestern periphery of the \nridge will continue to try to build over South Florida on Tuesday \nand Wednesday. At the surface, high pressure centered over the \nwestern Atlantic will continue to build over the Florida Peninsula\nand into the eastern Gulf heading towards the middle of the week.\nThis will allow for a return to a rather light southeasterly wind\nflow on Tuesday, and then a light to moderate easterly flow \nheading into the middle of the week. \n\nPlenty of moisture will linger across the region as the latest \nglobal and ensemble models continue to hint at relatively high PWAT \nvalues remaining in place through the middle of the week. With \nridging trying to build back into the region, the main driver of \nconvective initiation on Tuesday and Wednesday will be the sea\nbreeze boundaries as they push inland. Scattered to numerous \nshowers and thunderstorms will focus mainly over the interior and \nSouthwest Florida each afternoon during this time frame. However,\nwith the lighter southeasterly flow on Tuesday, showers and \nstorms may linger a bit longer near portions of the east coast \nmetro areas during the afternoon. High temperatures on Tuesday and\nWednesday should rise into the lower 90s across most areas with \npeak heat index values reaching 103 to 108 each day.\n\nMoving into Thursday and Friday, the forecast does become more \nuncertain as the latest global and ensemble guidance continues to \nshow signs of a potential TUTT feature moving over the Bahamas on \nThursday before potentially getting closer to the region on Friday. \nThe latest global and ensemble guidance suite still remains in \ndisagreement in regards to the exact timing and location of this \nfeature. The ECMWF guidance suite does show a stronger TUTT \ngetting a bit closer to the region, while the GFS suite shows this\nfeature remaining a bit further off to the northeast of the area\nand weaker. The closer and stronger this feature is, the bigger \nrole it could play in enhancing shower and thunderstorm activity \nduring this time frame. In any event, easterly flow still looks to\nhold strong heading into the end of the week, which would create \na focus of the highest shower and thunderstorm chances occurring \nover the interior and Southwest Florida each afternoon during this\ntime frame. The latest forecast takes a blend of the models and \nkeeps the highest chances of showers and storms over these areas \non Thursday and Friday. This will continue to be monitored as the\nweek progresses. High temperatures towards the end of the week \nwill rise into the lower 90s across most areas.",
